## What this repository holds

A **UEL model** at `{{PROJECT_REL}}`: a content-addressed, typed graph of
engineering claims — components exchanging conserved quantities across ports,
analyses justifying design decisions, and assumption envelopes calibrated by
test data. The model is the source of truth. Documents (BOMs, ICDs, travelers,
status) are **compiled projections**, never authored artifacts.

```
{{PROJECT_REL}}/
  uel.toml          project manifest + hash tolerances
  model/*.uel       requirements, components, bindings, analyses
  analysis/*.py     opaque cores (JSON in, JSON out — docs/core-protocol.md)
  test/*.json       measurement campaigns; reality writes back through these
  calibration/      overlays (as-designed + per-serial as-built), candidate rules
  uel.lock          recorded recipes, outputs, provenance — commit it
  out/              compiled projections — never hand-edit, always regenerate
```

## The five commands

```sh
uel agenda {{PROJECT_REL}}                  # what matters most right now
uel check {{PROJECT_REL}}                   # compile time: seconds, no solver
uel build {{PROJECT_REL}}                   # run the stale set in dependency order
uel query info-value {{PROJECT_REL}}        # which measurement buys the most
uel calibrate <campaign.json> {{PROJECT_REL}}   # reality writes back
uel doctor {{PROJECT_REL}}                  # checkup: drift, debt, and kernel defects
```

## How work happens here

The protocol is `docs/org/loop.md`, invocable as the `uel-loop` skill: read the
agenda, take the top item, gate it, judge it, record the cost. A SessionStart
hook prints the agenda when you arrive; a PostToolUse hook runs `uel check`
whenever you edit a `.uel` file and hands you the diagnostics immediately.

## What the graph guarantees, and what it does not

**Guaranteed mechanically:** units and dimensions, port conservation, budget
rollups with margins, envelope containment (a producer's guarantee must cover
its consumer's assumption), DFM rules for bound processes, and exact staleness
— change an input and precisely its downstream cone flips stale.

**Not guaranteed, ever:** that the design is *safe*. The checker enforces
**declared** assumptions; the dominant cause of loss in complex systems is
interaction nobody declared. A green check means the declared world is
consistent. Say that plainly when it matters — the green checkmark is never
sold as safety.

## Non-negotiables

- Units on every physical quantity. Bare numbers are a bug.
- References, never copies — copies do not carry staleness.
- Every assumption declared, with rationale, including unverifiable ones.
- Every computed result judged: is it physical, converged, sufficient, and does
  it answer the intent? Record the residual doubts.
- Never commit a red `uel check`. Never hand-edit `out/` — projections carry a
  digest of their own body, so `uel doctor` can prove an edit happened, and a
  doctored traveler on the floor is a safety problem, not a formatting one.
- UEL kernel defects become issues upstream (`uel-doctor` skill), never local
  patches. The upstream repo is public: file the redacted report unless the
  user has said this model can be disclosed.
